#{[17592186498478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498478 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "effd6099-f4df-5d9b-a019-343ae34a858c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498504 #edb/ref 1071 "A sports shop sells table tennis balls in boxes of $6.$ The weight of a table tennis ball, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $2.72\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $0.05\\,\\text{g},$ while the weight of a box, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $12\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{g}.$ If one box full of balls is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs less than $30\\,\\text{g}?$\nYou may assume that the weights are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "227f2a32-4a18-5560-9e21-65c9391f9977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498504 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th ball, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a box. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(2.72,{0.05}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 6,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(12,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected box of balls. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{6}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =6\\cdot 2.72+12 \\\\  & =28.32 \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =6\\cdot {0.05}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.015.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(28.32,4.015).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<30),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & =P(Z<\\frac{30-28.32}{\\sqrt{4.015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.84) \\\\  & =Φ(0.84)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.84)≈0.7995.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & ≈Φ(0.84) \\\\  & ≈0.7995.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498509 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(5,{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 14,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(70\\le Y\\le 78),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bacd74e-2117-5657-b43b-3639319ee133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498509 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14} & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(70,56).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(70\\le Y\\le 78).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(70\\le Y\\le 78) & =P(\\frac{70-70}{\\sqrt{56}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{78-70}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le 1.07) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.07)=0.8577.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(70\\le Y\\le 78)=Φ(1.07)-0.5=0.8577-0.5=0.3577.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498532 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 18,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(16\\le Y\\le 20),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bd1cff1-6c69-5338-8c54-dfce19778dbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498532 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18} & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =N(18,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(16\\le Y\\le 20).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16\\le Y\\le 20) & =P(\\frac{16-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{20-18}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{3}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.67\\le Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z\\ge 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2(1-P(Z\\le 0.67)) \\\\  & =2P(Z\\le 0.67))-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.67)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.67)=0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(16\\le Y\\le 20)=2Φ(0.67)-1=0.4972.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498549 #edb/ref 1071 "A bouquet consists of $20$ roses. The weight of a rose, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $70\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\text{g}.$ If one bouquet is randomly selected, what is the probability that it will weigh more than $1\\,430\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of the roses in a bouquet are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77a63100-6b22-5680-ab48-c534778dd5f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498549 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th rose. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(70,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected bouquet. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 70 \\\\  & =1\\,400, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\  & =500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,400,500).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>1\\,430),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & =P(Z>\\frac{1\\,430-1\\,400}{\\sqrt{500}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.34) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.34) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.34)≈0.9099.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & ≈1-Φ(1.34) \\\\  & ≈0.0901.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498564 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\frac{5}{2},{1}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 10,\n$$\n\nwhat is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99847d4a-0d57-508f-b2c0-9d057142ac5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498564 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10} & ∼N(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =N(25,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498569 #edb/ref 1071 "In a certain shop, eggs are sold in cartons by the dozen.\nThe weight of an egg, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $65\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\text{g},$ while the weight of an egg carton, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\text{g}.$\nIf one carton filled with eggs is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs more than $850\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f941d6e-0531-597c-97c5-4e948faf202b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498569 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th egg, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a carton. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(65,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 12,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(50,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected carton filled with eggs. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{12}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =12\\cdot 65+50 \\\\  & =830, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =12\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =304.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(830,304).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>850),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & =P(Z>\\frac{850-830}{\\sqrt{304}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.15) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.15)≈0.8749.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & ≈1-Φ(1.15) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8749 \\\\  & =0.1251.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498581 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(-0.05,{0.1}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 100,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\ge -4.25),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd6eb665-f20f-5952-b033-241935d01495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498581 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100} & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot {0.1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot 0.01) \\\\  & =N(-5,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge -4.25).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.25) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.25-(-5)}{\\sqrt{1}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.75) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.25)=1-Φ(0.75)=0.2266.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
